Problem statement:
Given an array of integers nums, rearrange the elements into the next lexicographically greater permutation of numbers.
If such an arrangement is not possible, rearrange the array into the lowest possible order (ascending order).

Example:
Input: nums = [1, 2, 3]
Output: [1, 3, 2]


Brute Force Approach : Generate All Permutations
The basic idea is to generate all possible permutations of the given array, arrange them in lexicographical order, and find the immediate next permutation.

Algorithm
1. Generate all possible permutations of the given array.
2. Sort all permutations in lexicographical order.
3. Find the position of the original array.
4. Select the permutation immediately after it.
5. If the original array is the last permutation, select the first permutation.

Code:


class Solution {
public:
    void nextPermutation(vector<int>& nums) {

        vector<int> original = nums;
        vector<vector<int>> perms;

        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        do {
            perms.push_back(nums);
        } while(next_permutation(nums.begin(), nums.end()));

        for(int i = 0; i < perms.size(); i++) {

            if(perms[i] == original) {

                if(i == perms.size() - 1)
                    nums = perms[0];
                else
                    nums = perms[i + 1];

                return;
            }
        }
    }
};

Time Complexity: (O(n.n!.log(n!))
Space Complexity: ((O)n.n!)


Optimal Approach : Pivot, Swap and Reverse
Observation 1: Find the longest prefix match
Find the first element from the right satisfying:
nums[i] < nums[i+1]
This position is called the pivot or breakpoint.

Observation 2: Find the smallest greater element
Find the smallest element greater than the pivot from the elements to its right.
Swap these two elements.

Observation 3: Arrange the remaining suffix in ascending order
Reverse the suffix after the pivot position to obtain the smallest possible arrangement.

Algorithm
1. Traverse the array from right to left and find the pivot.
2. If no pivot exists, reverse the entire array and return.
3. Traverse from right to left again and find the first element greater than the pivot.
4. Swap the pivot and the selected element.
5. Reverse the suffix after the pivot.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    void nextPermutation(vector<int>& nums) {

        int n = nums.size();
        int ind = -1;

        // Step 1: Find the pivot
        for(int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {

            if(nums[i] < nums[i + 1]) {
                ind = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Handle the largest permutation
        if(ind == -1) {
            reverse(nums.begin(), nums.end());
            return;
        }

        // Step 3: Find the smallest greater element
        for(int i = n - 1; i > ind; i--) {

            if(nums[i] > nums[ind]) {
                swap(nums[i], nums[ind]);
                break;
            }
        }

        // Step 4: Reverse the suffix
        reverse(nums.begin() + ind + 1, nums.end());
    }
};

Time Complexity: O(n)
Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1)
